  "GitHub_metadata": {
    "baseRefName": "main",
    "body": "## Result\n\nComplete counterexample to Krener's negative-curvature observability conjecture in its original smooth-manifold formulation.\n\nTake a connected double cover of a closed hyperbolic surface, observe it through the covering projection followed by a smooth Nash isometric embedding into R17, and use zero dynamics. The exact finite-time observability Gramian is the pulled-back hyperbolic metric: complete, uniformly bounded and positive definite, with curvature \u22121 at T=1. Each attained output history has exactly two initial states.\n\nThe source allows a general state manifold and does not require simple connectivity or nonzero dynamics. The package includes both intrinsic uniform bounds and explicit finite-atlas bounds. It does not claim a stronger result for a separately restricted Euclidean state domain.\n\n## Independent validation\n\n- Separate adversarial AI review: PASS_COMPLETE_COUNTEREXAMPLE\n- 873 submitted exact controls and 108 independent controls pass\n- Final proof SHA256: e22eb257358431f1c7e4dbdb2c8550c26972da1b24a573581aed8f84c14db0dd\n- Review SHA256: 42b19dec4300dffef93f5ff0e77fde412e36a88ac9903e0d9bee6a3e5c908988\n- One substantive construction family\n- Classical covering theory and Nash's smooth theorem are credited; historical priority remains unconfirmed\n- This has not undergone human peer review\n\nThe complete source contribution, dependency statements, proof, exact controls and independent review are recorded under unsolved_math_prioritization/attempts/30001203/. Imported record30001204 is the same source question, not a second result. No third-party PDFs or authentication data are included.\n",
